# PART 2 – The claim, its estimand and its causal graph {#part-2-the-claim-its-estimand-and-its-causal-graph .darkslide background-color="rgba(0,0,0,0)"}

::: {.partlabel}
Part 2 · 90 minutes
:::

::: notes
Part two. The case of the day is a survey study, and a careful one: more than a hundred thousand respondents in sixteen countries, asked how much they trust their parliament and what they think immigration does to their country. Five analysts in Multi100 reanalysed one of its claims from the same file, all five found evidence for it, and still their estimates differed by a factor of two. This part explains how that can happen. We go inside the paper first, then step back to two studies that show how often disagreement among analysts is disagreement about the question. Then we build the two tools that state a question precisely, the estimand and the causal graph, and use both on our claim, where the graph asks what each control in the model actually is.
:::

## The question of the paper {#the-question-of-the-paper .smaller}

::: {.small}
- **The argument.** Modern states rest on a shared national community, and many citizens regard immigration as a threat to it. Those most concerned "may believe that the political system (the elites and institutions) have sold out the public by failing to protect the national community" from immigration (p. 205)
- **The expectation.** "individuals expressing most concern about the impact of immigration on the national community will be most distrusting of politicians and political institutions" (Proposition 1, p. 205)
- **Two worries built into the design.** The association might be spurious, if "some individuals are simply more negatively disposed toward most ideas they encounter" (p. 209); and distrust of parliament might only be dislike of the government of the day
:::

::: {.fragment .small}
The paper was published in 2012 in *World Politics* [@McLaren2012CulturalDivideEurope]. From here on I call its author **the original author (OA)**.
:::

::: notes
The case of the day is a paper in World Politics, and I will call its author the original author, or OA, from here on, because the course is about the claim and its analyses rather than about the person who made it. The argument starts from the observation that European states were built on a shared sense of national community, and that many of their citizens now regard immigration as a threat to it. Those who worry most, the paper argues, may feel that the political class has sold them out, and so trust it less. Two worries shaped the design, and both come back as decisions on our menu: that the association is spurious, because some people are negative about everything, and that distrust of parliament is only dislike of whoever governs at the moment.
:::

## The data and the measures {#the-data-and-the-measures .smaller}

:::: {.columns}
::: {.column width="50%"}
::: {.small}
- The **European Social Survey** (ESS), rounds 1–4, with fieldwork from 2002–2003 to 2008–2009: a new random sample of adults in every country and round
- **16 countries** of Western Europe in 60 country-rounds; the newer democracies of Central and Eastern Europe are left out
- **120,080 respondents**, of whom the paper keeps the **110,732** citizens who do not belong to an ethnic minority, because the argument concerns members of the national community
:::
:::
::: {.column width="50%"}
::: {.small}
- **Distrust** of parliament, of politicians and of the legal system: each trust item (0–10) turned round, so that 10 means no trust at all; the paper keeps the three apart
- **Concern about immigration**: the mean of three items, each turned round – immigration is bad for the economy, undermines cultural life, makes the country a worse place to live
- Cronbach's alpha of the three items: **0.84** on the course extract, against an average of 0.83 in the paper
:::
:::
::::

::: {.src}
[@McLaren2012CulturalDivideEurope, pp. 212–213 and 231–232]; cases/trust/data/trust_ess.csv and display_reliability.csv.
:::

::: notes
The data are the first four rounds of the European Social Survey, which interviews a fresh random sample of adults in every country every two years. The paper uses sixteen countries of Western Europe and leaves out the newer democracies of the East, where questions about immigrants would be heard as questions about neighbours across a changed border. It also keeps only citizens who do not belong to an ethnic minority, because the argument is about members of the national community, which leaves a hundred and ten thousand respondents. Distrust is measured three times, for parliament, politicians and the legal system, each trust item turned round. Concern is the mean of three items about the economy, cultural life and the country as a place to live, and the three hang together well, with an alpha of point eight four.
:::

