## Theoretical and empirical estimands {#what-is-your-estimand}


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![](trust-one-day_files/figure-revealjs/lundberg-pipeline-1.png){width=864}
:::
:::


::: {.none}
- The theoretical estimand is stated **outside** any model – before R opens
:::

::: {.src}
@LundbergEtAl2021WhatYourEstimand.
:::

::: notes
Our five analysts and the twenty-nine red-card teams were all handed a question in words, and none of them was told exactly which quantity would answer it. Lundberg and colleagues call that quantity the estimand, and the title of their paper asks for it directly: "What is your estimand?" They split the answer into three steps. First, the theoretical estimand: a unit-specific quantity and a target population, stated in plain words, before any model exists. Second, the empirical estimand, which is what in the observable data corresponds to that quantity – and making that link forces your identification assumptions into the open, which is the job of the causal graph. Only third comes estimation, where R finally opens. When they audited a full year of papers in a top sociology journal, the unit quantity was clearly stated in five out of thirty-two. By their standard, every quantitative study must be able to answer that question before R opens.
:::

## Confounders, mediators and colliders {#dag-crash-course-three-roles .smaller}


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![](trust-one-day_files/figure-revealjs/mini-dags-1.png){width=912}
:::
:::


::: {.src}
X = predictor, Y = outcome. **Green** = the causal effect you want; **coloured** = the path you must reason about.
:::

::: notes
The causal graph is the second of those three steps, and these three small graphs cover all the graph theory that the tasks need. In every panel the green arrow is the same thing, the effect of X on Y that you are actually after. What changes is the coloured structure around it. A confounder is a common cause of your predictor and your outcome – that red fork is a back-door path, and if you leave it open you are biased, so you adjust to close it. A mediator lies on the causal path between them. The amber path is real and part of the total effect, so if the total effect is what you want you do not adjust for it, or you remove part of the very thing you are measuring. And a collider is caused by both sides – those edges are harmless until you condition on the collider, which opens a spurious path that was not there before. So the rulebook is short: adjust confounders, leave mediators alone for total effects, and never condition on a collider. The next four slides add a little depth to those three rules before you use them.
:::

## Causal stories behind a correlation {#one-number-many-stories .smaller}

:::: {.columns}
::: {.column width="54%"}


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![](trust-one-day_files/figure-revealjs/many-stories-1.png){width=499.2}
:::
:::


:::
::: {.column width="46%"}
::: {.small}
The same slope fits four different stories:

::: {.fragment}
- X really causes Y – the slope *is* the effect
:::
::: {.fragment}
- a common cause inflates it – **confounding**
:::
::: {.fragment}
- the sample itself is selected – **collider / selection**
:::
::: {.fragment}
- Y causes X (**reverse causation**), or it is **chance**
:::
::: {.fragment}
The data look identical in all four. Only an assumption about *how the data arose* can tell them apart, and a DAG is where you write that assumption down.
:::
:::
:::
::::

::: {.src}
@DAgostinoMcGowanEtAl2024CausalInferenceNot; @KeeleEtAl2020CausalInterpretationEstimated. Scatter simulated for illustration.
:::

::: notes
The scatter here has a strong correlation, an r of about nought point six. Before you reach for a model, consider that this single number is consistent with at least four different stories. Maybe X really does cause Y, and the slope is the effect. Maybe a common cause is inflating it. Maybe we are quietly looking at a selected sample, and the association is an artefact of who got in. Maybe Y causes X, or it is simply chance. Nothing in the data can decide between these, because the scatter looks identical in every case. Only a claim about how the data were generated can do that. A DAG is where you write that claim down, and that is why we draw one before we fit anything.
:::

## Reading a DAG: open and closed paths {.smaller}


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![](trust-one-day_files/figure-revealjs/backdoor-paths-1.png){width=912}
:::
:::


::: {.small}
Association flows along **every open path**, not only along the causal arrow. A fork or a chain is open until you adjust for the variable in its middle. A collider is the reverse: shut by default, and opened when you adjust for it.

- **Back-door criterion**: close every path that enters X from behind, keep the causal X → Y path open
- Only an adjustment set that does this supports a causal reading
:::

::: {.src}
@HernanRobins2020CausalInferenceWhat; @Huntington-Klein2021EffectIntroductionResearch.
:::

::: notes
A DAG is read through its paths. Association flows along every open path between X and Y, not only down the causal arrow. So the job is to work out which paths are open and shut the ones you do not want. Two rules do all the work. A path through a fork or a chain is open, and you close it by adjusting the variable in the middle – that is the red back-door fork on the left. A path through a collider is the opposite. It is shut by default, and you open it by adjusting. The back-door criterion puts the two rules together: close every path that sneaks into X from behind, and leave the causal X-to-Y path alone. Only an adjustment set that does that gives you an estimate you are allowed to read as causal.
:::

## Total effect or direct effect {#which-effect-do-you-even-want .smaller}

:::: {.columns}
::: {.column width="55%"}


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![](trust-one-day_files/figure-revealjs/total-direct-1.png){width=537.6}
:::
:::


:::
::: {.column width="45%"}
::: {.small}
- **Total** = direct (green) + indirect (amber)
- Adjust for the mediator M → you estimate the **direct** effect only – a *different* estimand
- 'Control for everything' can switch you to an estimand you never chose
- **Table 2 fallacy**: the other coefficients in your model are *not* all causal effects
:::
:::
::::

::: {.src}
@KeeleEtAl2020CausalInterpretationEstimated; @DAgostinoMcGowanEtAl2024CausalInferenceNot.
:::

::: notes
Suppose M lies between your predictor and your outcome. The total effect is the green arrow plus the amber route through M. The direct effect is just the green arrow. These are different numbers and different questions – and which one you get depends entirely on whether you put M in the model. Drop a mediator in as just another 'control' and you have silently switched to the direct effect without telling anyone, including yourself. This is why 'control for everything' is bad advice. And it has a famous corollary, the Table 2 fallacy. When you print a regression table, usually only one coefficient has a clean causal reading. The coefficients on the control variables are not little causal effects of their own.
:::

## Selection and feedback {#spurious-by-design-selection-feedback .smaller}


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![](trust-one-day_files/figure-revealjs/selection-panel-1.png){width=960}
:::
:::


::: {.small}
- **Selection / collider bias** – conditioning, or merely *sampling*, on a common effect manufactures an association that was never there (much of the early COVID risk-factor literature)
- **Reverse causation & feedback** – in a panel, which follows the same units over time, today's outcome can move tomorrow's predictor; adjusting for the stable traits of each unit does **not** remove that feedback, and a randomised experiment avoids it by design
:::

::: {.src}
@GriffithEtAl2020ColliderBiasUndermines; @ColeEtAl2010IllustratingBiasDue; @LeszczenskyWolbring2022HowDealReverse.
:::

::: notes
Two last ways in which a relationship can be spurious. The first is selection, or collider bias. If you condition on – or merely sample on – something that both your variables cause, you manufacture a correlation out of nothing. A lot of the early COVID risk-factor findings were of this kind, drawn from people who had been tested, which is a selected group. Hold on to this one, because it comes back in our own case in a form that is easy to miss. The second is reverse causation and feedback. In a panel, which follows the same people or places over time, today's outcome can feed tomorrow's predictor, and adjusting for the stable differences between units does not, on its own, fix a confounder that changes over time or a feedback loop. A randomised experiment escapes this second problem by design, because nothing that happens later can change who received which treatment.
:::

